## 1. Where you are, who else is on the machine, what you never touch

- You start in `{{BASE}}`, the base worktree of `{{REPO}}` on `{{BASE_BRANCH}}`.
  `{{BASE}}` is a BASE ONLY. You do not develop in it. Every story gets its own
  worktree under `{{NIGHT_DIR}}/wt/<id>/` (section 3).
- The night run's own state lives in `{{NIGHT_DIR}}`: `queue.txt`, `state.txt`,
  `worktrees.tsv`, `logs/`, `run.lock`, `STOP`. You append to your own rows and
  your own log. You never edit another story's rows, and you never delete
  `run.lock` or `STOP`.
- This is a SHARED machine running several unrelated services and, possibly,
  other Claude sessions. Before any port, container, service or reverse-proxy
  change: read `~/infra/INVENTORY.md` and `~/infra/PORTS.md`. Never stop a
  container that does not belong to {{PROJECT}}. If you change machine state,
  log it with `update-monitor note "..."` in English.
- Never run `git` in `{{REPO}}` for anything except `fetch`, `worktree`,
  `branch` and `tag`. If `{{REPO}}` is also a deployed tree, development in it
  can ship untested code to a live service — so: worktrees are created FROM it,
  never developed IN it.
- Never touch a worktree this story did not create. Another story, another
  session or another project may be living in it, with uncommitted work in it.
- Never merge, rebase, close or comment on a PR you did not open.
- Never edit `.github/**`, CI workflow files, secrets, credentials, `.env`
  files, or anything `{{NIGHT_RULES}}` (section 6) lists as forbidden. If a
  story appears to require a change there, that story is BLOCKED: say so in the
  PR body and in your report, and do not do it anyway.
- Never install or enable a systemd unit, timer, cron entry or service on the
  machine. Writing unit files INTO the repo is fine; activating them is the
  owner's, in daylight.

