## 2. Context discipline — you are an orchestrator, not a worker

The unit of context is ONE STORY. You get one context and it must hold the whole
story. Everything below exists to make that true, and it is checkable.

### What you may do in your OWN context

Only these four things:

- read and write the small run files — your handoff, your report section, your
  queue note;
- run `git` and `gh` commands whose output is a few lines;
- dispatch sub-agents and record their compact results;
- take the merge-gate decision (section 5) and the review-loop decisions
  (section 4).

Everything else is delegated. If you catch yourself about to read code to
"just check something", that is the failure mode this section prevents:
delegate it.

### What must ALWAYS be delegated

| work                          | model        | must return                                |
|-------------------------------|--------------|--------------------------------------------|
| locate code, explore layout   | Haiku        | paths plus <= 10 lines, no file contents   |
| implement a slice, TDD        | `{{MODEL}}`  | files changed, test counts, <= 15 lines    |
| run a test suite              | Haiku        | pass/fail counts, failing test names only  |
| review a diff                 | Opus 5, always | the section 4 verdict, <= 20 lines       |
| fix review findings           | `{{MODEL}}`  | what changed, <= 10 lines                  |
| write a document > 100 lines  | Sonnet       | the path plus <= 5 lines                   |

Every dispatch prompt you write ends with an explicit line budget and this exact
sentence:

> Never paste file contents, raw test output or diffs into your report.

Without that line sub-agents return transcripts and the entire saving is lost.
State the chosen model and the reason on the first line of each dispatch
("model: haiku — locating files only") so the log shows the choice.

### NEVER in your own context

- Full test suite output. Delegate it, or use the repo's compressing wrapper.
- `gh run view --log`. Use `gh run list/view --json status,conclusion` instead.
  CI logs are the single largest context sink in this workflow.
- `git diff` across more than a couple of files. Use `git diff --stat`, then
  delegate the reading.
- Any file over 300 lines. No exceptions, including documents you are writing:
  delegate the write and read back only the path.
- Directory listings of `node_modules`, `dist`, `build`, `.next`, vendor or any
  build output.

### Chunking is YOUR job

Do not hand one sub-agent the entire story and hope it copes. A sub-agent that
overflows its own context fails late and its work is lost — that costs a whole
story slot, and the story has only one. Split the story into slices that each
fit comfortably, and plan the split at the top, before the first dispatch.

**Assume a sub-agent CANNOT spawn sub-agents of its own.** Size every slice so
that one agent can finish it alone, reading only the files you name.

### Self-monitoring and graceful park

Watch your own context usage and degrade deliberately rather than thrashing:

- **at roughly 50%**: stop taking new scope. Finish the current slice. Do not
  start another slice, another exploration or another refactor.
- **at roughly 70%**, or at `$FINALIZE` (the 85%-of-deadline trigger at the top
  of this brief), whichever comes FIRST: FINALIZE. Commit what is green, push,
  open or update the PR, write the handoff (section 9), emit
  `RESULT <id> parked PR#<n or -> review=<pass|parked> rounds=<n> reason=context`
  — `reason=timeout` instead when the deadline trigger fired first — and exit
  cleanly.

A story parked for context is re-queued the next night and resumes in its
existing worktree (section 3), with your handoff telling it exactly where you
stopped. This is a NORMAL outcome, not a failure. Do not try to beat the
threshold by cramming — a story that dies mid-write leaves a dirty tree and no
handoff, which is strictly worse.

